# Deploy to Google Kubernetes Engine

This guide deploys Employee Management to a Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) Autopilot cluster. It stores the application images in Artifact Registry and exposes only the frontend publicly. The mock API remains private inside the cluster.

## Prerequisites

Install and authenticate the Google Cloud CLI, Docker, and kubectl. You need a Google Cloud project with billing enabled.

```bash
gcloud auth login
gcloud auth application-default login
```

Set the following values for your environment:

```bash
export PROJECT_ID="your-gcp-project-id"
export REGION="us-central1"
export REPOSITORY="employee-app"
export CLUSTER="employee-cluster"
export VERSION="v1"

gcloud config set project "$PROJECT_ID"
```

## Enable GCP services

```bash
gcloud services enable container.googleapis.com artifactregistry.googleapis.com
```

## Create the Artifact Registry repository

Run this once per project and region:

```bash
gcloud artifacts repositories create "$REPOSITORY" \
  --repository-format=docker \
  --location="$REGION" \
  --description="Employee Management container images"

gcloud auth configure-docker "$REGION-docker.pkg.dev"
```

## Build and publish images

```bash
export IMAGE_PREFIX="$REGION-docker.pkg.dev/$PROJECT_ID/$REPOSITORY"

docker build -t "$IMAGE_PREFIX/employee-app:$VERSION" .
docker push "$IMAGE_PREFIX/employee-app:$VERSION"

docker build -t "$IMAGE_PREFIX/employee-mock-api:$VERSION" -f Dockerfile.mock .
docker push "$IMAGE_PREFIX/employee-mock-api:$VERSION"
```

Use a new version tag for every release, such as `v2`. Do not reuse `latest` for deployments.

## Create and connect to GKE

Create an Autopilot cluster, or skip the creation command when using an existing cluster:

```bash
gcloud container clusters create-auto "$CLUSTER" --region="$REGION"

gcloud container clusters get-credentials "$CLUSTER" --region="$REGION"
kubectl cluster-info
```

## Configure the manifests

Update the `image` fields in `kubernetes/app.yaml` and `kubernetes/mock-api.yaml` before deployment:

```yaml
# kubernetes/app.yaml
image: REGION-docker.pkg.dev/PROJECT_ID/REPOSITORY/employee-app:VERSION

# kubernetes/mock-api.yaml
image: REGION-docker.pkg.dev/PROJECT_ID/REPOSITORY/employee-mock-api:VERSION
```

Replace `REGION`, `PROJECT_ID`, `REPOSITORY`, and `VERSION` with the values used above. Keep the mock API Service name as `mock-api`; the Nginx frontend proxies `/api` requests to that internal DNS name.

Change the frontend Service in `kubernetes/app.yaml` from `ClusterIP` to `LoadBalancer`:

```yaml
type: LoadBalancer
```

Leave the mock API Service as `ClusterIP` so it is not publicly exposed.

## Deploy and verify

```bash
kubectl apply -k kubernetes
kubectl rollout status deployment/employee-app
kubectl rollout status deployment/mock-api
kubectl get pods
kubectl get service employee-app --watch
```

Wait for `employee-app` to receive an `EXTERNAL-IP`, then open `http://EXTERNAL-IP` in a browser. Press `Ctrl+C` to stop watching the Service without affecting the deployment.

## Update a release

Build and push both images using a new version tag, update the two image values in the manifests, then apply the changes:

```bash
kubectl apply -k kubernetes
kubectl rollout status deployment/employee-app
kubectl rollout status deployment/mock-api
```

## Remove the application

```bash
kubectl delete -k kubernetes
```

To avoid ongoing charges, delete resources that are no longer needed:

```bash
gcloud container clusters delete "$CLUSTER" --region="$REGION"
gcloud artifacts repositories delete "$REPOSITORY" --location="$REGION"
```